  "full_response": "WebSockets and HTTP long polling are both techniques used to enable real-time communication between a client (e.g., a web browser) and a server, but they work in fundamentally different ways. Here’s a breakdown of each and their key differences:\n\n---\n\n### **1. WebSockets**\n**What it is:**\nWebSockets is a **full-duplex, bidirectional communication protocol** that enables persistent, low-latency connections between a client and a server over a single TCP connection. It is designed for real-time applications where the server needs to push updates to the client instantly (e.g., chat apps, live sports scores, stock tickers, or multiplayer games).\n\n**How it works:**\n- The client initiates a WebSocket handshake via an HTTP `Upgrade` request (e.g., `GET /chat` with `Upgrade: websocket`).\n- If the server supports WebSockets, it responds with `HTTP 101 Switching Protocols`, and the connection switches from HTTP to the WebSocket protocol.\n- After the handshake, both the client and server can send messages to each other **independently and asynchronously** without re-establishing the connection.\n- The connection remains open until either the client or server terminates it.\n\n**Key features:**\n- **Persistent connection:** No need to repeatedly open/close connections.\n- **Low latency:** Messages are sent immediately without waiting for a request.\n- **Efficient:** Minimal overhead after the initial handshake (no HTTP headers for each message).\n- **Bidirectional:** Both client and server can send messages at any time.\n- **Supports binary data:** Can send text (e.g., JSON) or binary (e.g., images, audio).\n\n**Example use cases:**\n- Chat applications (e.g., Slack, Discord).\n- Live notifications (e.g., Facebook, Twitter).\n- Online gaming.\n- Financial trading platforms.\n\n**Limitations:**\n- More complex to implement than HTTP.\n- Requires server-side support for WebSockets.\n- Firewalls/proxies may block WebSocket traffic (though this is rare today).\n\n---\n\n### **2. HTTP Long Polling**\n**What it is:**\nHTTP long polling is a **workaround** to simulate real-time communication using standard HTTP requests. It is a variation of traditional polling where the client sends a request to the server, and the server holds the request open until new data is available or a timeout occurs.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. The client sends an HTTP",
